  "notes": [
    "The corpus is synthetic. These numbers show the system behaves as designed on cases whose ground truth is known by construction; they do not show it works on real-world noise, because the corpus contains none.",
    "Run against the deterministic mock provider, so cost is genuinely $0.00 and latency measures Bishop's own code rather than a model round trip. Verdicts come from arithmetic over detector scores \u2014 see src/bishop/models/mock.py.",
    "20 alerts is a smoke test, not a benchmark. One alert moving changes accuracy by 5 points.",
    "This is the held-out set. It was written after the fusion thresholds were fixed, nothing here was used to tune anything, and it is run separately from `just eval` so it cannot leak into the loop by habit. Read this number, not the golden-set one, when asking whether Bishop generalises.",
    "Several cases here describe techniques Bishop has no detector for \u2014 Kerberoasting, cloud token theft. The correct behaviour on those is to escalate, so a low accuracy driven by escalations is a different and much less serious result than one driven by missed true positives. Read the false-negative rate and the confusion matrix, not the headline.",
    "If a case here gets fixed, it stops being held out \u2014 debugging against it converts it into a development case. The honest move is to move it into fixtures/alerts/ and write a fresh held-out case, not to keep the label and the credit.",
    "Technique recall is 58%: some techniques a labelled alert should surface are not produced by any detector. That gap is real and docs/COVERAGE.md shows exactly where it is."
  ]
